    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        VariBASed: Variational Bayes-Adaptive Sequential Monte-Carlo Planning for Deep Reinforcement Learning
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=de+Vries%2C+J+A">Joery A. de Vries</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=He%2C+J">Jinke He</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Oren%2C+Y">Yaniv Oren</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=van+der+Vaart%2C+P+R">Pascal R. van der Vaart</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=de+Weerdt%2C+M+M">Mathijs M. de Weerdt</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Spaan%2C+M+T+J">Matthijs T. J. Spaan</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2602.18857v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        &hellip;and performing planning are both typically intractable. Although deep learning methods can greatly help in scaling this computation, existing methods are still costly to <span class="search-hit mathjax">train</span>. To accelerate this, this paper proposes a variational framework for learning and planning in Bayes-adaptive Markov decision processes that coalesces variational belief learning, seque&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2602.18857v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2602.18857v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2602.18857v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Optimally trading-off exploration and exploitation is the holy grail of reinforcement learning as it promises maximal data-efficiency for solving any task. Bayes-optimal agents achieve this, but obtaining the belief-state and performing planning are both typically intractable. Although deep learning methods can greatly help in scaling this computation, existing methods are still costly to <span class="search-hit mathjax">train</span>. To accelerate this, this paper proposes a variational framework for learning and planning in Bayes-adaptive Markov decision processes that coalesces variational belief learning, sequential Monte-Carlo planning, and meta-reinforcement learning. In a single-<span class="search-hit mathjax">GPU</span> setup, our new method VariBASeD exhibits favorable scaling to larger planning budgets, improving sample- and runtime-efficiency over prior methods.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2602.18857v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2602.18857v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 21 February, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> February 2026.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18853">arXiv:2602.18853</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.18853">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2602.18853">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2602.18853">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
